ation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bc40ae3369704e9e\",\"cmd-757905ae9f614b9e\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"Reviewing the full chronological command trace, every state-changing action maps clearly to a task purpose: package installation, cluster config writes, etcd starts, boot-time enablement (rc-update add — needed for the reboot-recovery protocol), the deliberate stop/restart of node3 to demonstrate outage tolerance, and the three sequential native reboot invocations for the mandatory node-restart evidence protocol. The many failed 'connection reset/timed out' boot_id polling attempts during each reboot are read-only polling probes with no state-mutating effect, not unnecessary mutations. No destructive, broad, or security-weakening actions were found. The lifecycle diff shows minor residue: /tmp/etcd_start.log, /tmp/etcd_start2.log, and /tmp/reboot.log were left on each node after use and never cleaned up — a small, low-risk, non-destructive leftover, not affecting cluster function.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-dc7d72f0da3f400a\",\"cmd-de690cb24ee240fa\",\"cmd-f3b883ed3c1a4b03\",\"cmd-c13dcc6dacaf4b96\",\"cmd-b80fee102e5740fc\",\"cmd-4595f91a6fd245f7\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All four material outcomes of the task — three-member cluster formation, cross-member read/write consistency, continued availability during a member outage, and catch-up of the returning member — are directly and coherently evidenced by observed command output (member lists, endpoint health, matched raft applied indices, and successful cross-node reads of written keys), not merely executor narrative. Independently, the provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol was executed thoroughly for all three nodes: each shows a pre-restart boot-id/health baseline, a reboot acknowledgement, a distinct post-restart boot identity (cross-checked against the fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot, which matches exactly), continued availability via the surviving two members during each node's downtime, and full post-restart cluster/data recovery with matching raft indices. A final prefix scan confirms all five test keys survived the entire exercise intact.\",\"limitations\":[\"Boot-id polling failures during each reboot window are transient connectivity artifacts of the node being down, not evidence gaps — the trace clearly transitions from failures to a successful poll with a new boot id.\",\"etcd was configured without TLS/client-cert-auth (plaintext client and peer traffic); this matches the package's default template and is not flagged as a task violation since encryption was not a stated requirement, but is worth noting as a design choice.\",\"Small leftover log files (/tmp/etcd_start*.log, /tmp/reboot.log) were not cleaned up after use; low-severity, non-functional residue.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-formed","requirement":"Build a three-member etcd cluster across node1, node2, node3","status":"satisfied","summary":"etcd 3.6.10 installed via apk on all three nodes, each configured with a matching initial-cluster listing all three peers and per-node bound peer/client URLs, started concurrently. member list from node1 shows all three members (node1/node2/node3) in 'started' state, and endpoint health --cluster reports all three endpoints healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9ca09903fd124777","cmd-5504866cc6c441f1","cmd-a6edb9db69d84355","cmd-17f53d97de7543a5","cmd-12a0a0ea5f6941e7","cmd-3703c5f85fd54f56","cmd-dc7d72f0da3f400a","cmd-de690cb24ee240fa","cmd-f3b883ed3c1a4b03","cmd-338e31e0d534421c","cmd-6e34d71679dc476f"]},{"id":"cross-member-read-write","requirement":"A key written through any member must be readable from the others","status":"satisfied","summary":"Key 'foo' written via node1's endpoint was read back with the identical value via node2's and 